89:     - Each gray bar 4-touches exactly one orange rectangle; some orange rectangles have no touching gray bar.
90:     - Gray bars touching the same orange rectangle all have the same orientation.
91: 
92:     Rule
93:     - Recolor every original gray cell to yellow.
94:     - For each orange rectangle touched by gray bars, count the gray cells assigned to that rectangle.
95:     - Horizontal gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange rows starting from the contacted top or bottom row.
96:     - Vertical gray bars repaint that many cells as complete orange columns starting from the contacted left or right column.
97:     - Orange rectangles with no touching gray bar remain unchanged.
98: 
99:     Key insight
100:     - The gray pixels are not copied by shape; their count is transferred into the neighboring orange rectangle as edge-aligned row or column stripes.
101:     """
102:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)
103:     assert g.ndim == 2, "expected a 2D grid"
104:     assert set(map(int, np.unique(g))) <= {Color.BLACK, Color.YELLOW, Color.GRAY, Color.ORANGE}
105: 
106:     orange = find_components(g, bg=Color.YELLOW, colors=[Color.ORANGE], connectivity=4)
107:     gray = find_components(g, bg=Color.YELLOW, colors=[Color.GRAY], connectivity=4)
108:     assert orange and gray, "expected orange rectangles and gray source bars"
109: 
110:     orange_index: dict[tuple[int, int], int] = {}
111:     orange_bboxes: list[tuple[int, int, int, int]] = []
112:     for i, obj in enumerate(orange):
